# Bandit configuration for media_conversion_bot.
#
# bandit auto-discovers this file in the working directory, and the Security Scan
# workflow also passes `--ini .bandit` explicitly. (`-c/--configfile` is a
# different flag — the YAML plugin profile — and this file is not that.) What
# follows is the recorded decision for every finding class this project has
# reviewed and accepted. Anything NOT skipped is still reported.
#
# The checks that matter for this codebase stay armed: B602/B604 (subprocess
# with shell=True), B603 (subprocess without shell=True — see below), B607
# (partial executable path), B101 (assert), B105/B106/B107 (hardcoded
# credentials), B324 (weak hash used for security), B301/B302 (pickle), B506
# (yaml.load), B608 (SQL assembled by formatting) and the rest of the B5xx
# injection family. Fixes landed alongside this file removed the only findings in
# those classes (B324 in utils/userbot_uploader.py, B607 in main.py,
# workers/ffmpeg_worker.py and utils/file_utils.py, B101 in utils/ffmpeg_runner.py,
# B105 in utils/job_access.py).
#
# B110/B112 mirror decisions already recorded in ruff.toml for the equivalent
# ruff rules (S110/S112). B404 is an import-level "consider the implications"
# note that fires on the import rather than on a call, so a per-line
# suppression would carry no information. Nothing about subprocess execution is
# skipped globally any more.

[bandit]
# B110 (try/except/pass) and B112 (try/except/continue)
#   This project's error handling is deliberately best-effort on cleanup and
#   probing paths: closing a Redis client, reading a cancel flag, stat-ing a file
#   that may have been removed, decoding a message that may be malformed. Letting
#   a failed teardown raise would be strictly worse than continuing.
#   ruff.toml records the same decision by ignoring S110 and S112. All 61 sites
#   were reviewed: none swallows an error around a security decision, and the
#   succeeding path already handles the absence it falls through to.
#
# B404 (import subprocess)
#   A design-level "consider the implications" note that fires on the import, not
#   on a call, so a per-line suppression would carry no information. The call
#   sites are the thing to review, and B603 was exactly that review.
#
# B603 (subprocess without shell=True) — deliberately NOT skipped.
#   It is an informational prompt to check for untrusted input, so it is
#   suppressed per call site (`# nosec B603` plus a reason) instead of globally.
#   That is the whole point: a new subprocess call anywhere in the tree is still
#   reported, which is the only way this check keeps earning its place. All 15
#   existing call sites were reviewed: every one passes a list argv and never a
#   shell string, and the argv is either a literal (["ps", "aux"],
#   [FFMPEG_PATH, "-version"]) or an ffmpeg/ffprobe argument list the worker
#   builds from validated paths. Verified:
#   `grep -rn "shell\s*=\s*True" --include=*.py .` returns nothing.
#   Each suppression names B603 and nothing else, on purpose: a bare `# nosec`
#   would also swallow a shell=True added to that same line later, so B602/B604
#   stay live on all 15 lines. For the same reason every rule in
#   security/scan_mediabot.py's injection family (INJ-*) refuses the generic
#   neutralizer and honours only a comment naming its own class, so annotating a
#   call for bandit cannot silently drop it out of injection coverage.
#
# In-code suppressions carry a reason after a second `#`. Two forms are in use:
#
#   * Bare `# nosec` — for a line that only ever trips ONE check, e.g.
#     `... # nosec  # noqa: S104 - container listener`. Five sites: the reviewed
#     bind-all-interfaces listeners (fetcher/service.py,
#     workers/ffmpeg_worker.py, tools/telethon_ingest.py), the protected-path
#     deny-list in utils/file_utils.py, the scheme-allowlisted urlopen calls in
#     scripts/check_forward_s3.py and scripts/trigger_upload_get.py, and the
#     login-flow field defaults in utils/login_handler.py.
#   * Named `# nosec B603` — for the 15 subprocess call sites, where naming the
#     id keeps B602/B604 (shell=True) armed on that same line.
#
# The reason must sit behind a second `#`. bandit captures everything up to the
# next `#` as candidate test ids and warns for every word it cannot resolve
# ("Test in comment: literal is not a test name or id"), so a bare
# `# nosec B603 - literal argv` turns one annotation into six log lines. With the
# reason behind the second `#` the whole repo produces 3 warnings, all benign:
# bandit runs its tests per Call node and a suppression matches any line in that
# node's line range, so an *outer* call whose range happens to contain the
# annotation reports that IT has no B603 to suppress (handlers.py:1450, the
# `asyncio.to_thread(` node, and twice on workers/ffmpeg_worker.py:2036 for the
# `str(` / `os.getpid(` nodes). Those are log noise, not findings: CI reads the
# JSON report, where the finding count is what gates the job.
skips = B110,B112,B404

# Not part of the shipped or reviewed surface:
#   security/        the audit scanner, whose rule tables deliberately contain
#                    secret-shaped literals and fixed-argv git/pip-audit calls
#   .vulnclaw-local/ vendored instance config and skill references
#   .venv,/venv,env  installed dependencies
#   __pycache__      compiled bytecode
#
# tests/ is deliberately NOT excluded. Excluding it made the directory a blind
# spot, and the one HIGH finding to ever come from there (B324, the md5 helper in
# tests/test_parallel_upload_streaming.py) stayed invisible to every local run
# until the CI job went red. It is scanned like any other directory, and the two
# classes of test-only noise are dealt with where each belongs:
#   B101 asserts     accepted by scripts/check_bandit.py, the only place that can
#                    scope a skip to tests/ - this file's `skips` are global, and
#                    skipping B101 here would also silence an assert in production
#                    code, where `python -O` deletes it.
#   fixture values   the B105/B106 hits on throwaway values carry a per-line
#                    `# nosec <id>` naming the check, the same way
#                    scripts/check_eventbus_integration.py does.
# Note: an `exclude` in this file REPLACES bandit's built-in default list rather
# than extending it, so the entries bandit ships with .git/__pycache__/.tox/.eggs
# are repeated here.
exclude = ./.git,./.venv,./venv,./env,./security,./.vulnclaw-local,./__pycache__,./.tox,./.eggs,./node_modules
